WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.731
A network has thousands of links coming up one by one, and you must keep answering: are these two servers connected yet? Union find answers almost instantly.

00:00:12.931 --> 00:00:13.860
Pause and think.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.242
What are the two speed-ups that make union find nearly constant time?

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:25.957
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:27.157 --> 00:00:36.097
This lesson is about union find, also called disjoint set union. It answers one question very fast: are these two things in the same group?

00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:40.500
Picture a school where every club has one captain.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:48.833
To ask whether two students are in the same club, you ask each of them: who is your captain? If the captains match, they are in the same club.

00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:54.113
To merge two clubs, one captain simply agrees to report to the other captain.

00:00:54.563 --> 00:01:02.330
That is the whole idea. Find means: follow the chain up to the captain. Union means: make one captain report to another.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.140
Here are six people, each starting as their own captain.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.119
We union one and two. Now one is the captain of two.

00:01:11.569 --> 00:01:16.654
We union three and four, then two and three. The groups join into one big tree.

00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:21.737
Five and six stay in their own small group, so find of five and find of one are different.

00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:26.802
In code, the whole structure is just one array called parent.

00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:35.264
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:01:36.874 --> 00:01:41.123
parent of i holds who i reports to. A captain points to itself.

00:01:41.573 --> 00:01:45.788
Find follows parent pointers until it reaches someone who points to themself.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:54.199
New word: pointer. A variable holding the memory address of something else. Picture it like this: A note saying "the book is on shelf 4".

00:01:55.849 --> 00:02:03.349
Path compression is a shortcut: on the way back, point every visited node straight at the captain, so the next find is one step.

00:02:03.482 --> 00:02:13.130
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:15.632 --> 00:02:17.489
Here are the words you will hear.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:21.144
A root, or representative, is the captain of a set.

00:02:21.594 --> 00:02:27.074
Union by rank or size attaches the smaller tree under the bigger one, which keeps trees short.

00:02:27.524 --> 00:02:36.835
With both tricks, each operation is almost constant time. The math name is inverse Ackermann, alpha of n, which is at most four for any real input.

00:02:38.185 --> 00:02:39.578
Where does this show up?

00:02:40.028 --> 00:02:45.357
Redundant Connection: an edge whose two ends already share a captain closes a cycle.

00:02:45.366 --> 00:02:55.745
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:02:57.395 --> 00:03:02.051
Accounts Merge: emails are the people, and sharing an account means union.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:10.755
In systems: connected components in a network, grouping crash reports by shared stack frames, and Kruskal's minimum spanning tree.

00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:19.928
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:03:22.478 --> 00:03:30.443
Remember: find walks up to the captain, union links two captains, and the two tricks make it nearly free. Try Redundant Connection next.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.684
Back to the question.

00:03:32.084 --> 00:03:36.066
What are the two speed-ups that make union find nearly constant time?

00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:39.136
Path compression and union by rank or size.

00:03:40.336 --> 00:03:41.207
Quick recap.

00:03:41.607 --> 00:03:43.859
Each item points toward its group's leader.

00:03:44.259 --> 00:03:46.222
Find follows pointers to the leader.

00:03:46.622 --> 00:03:48.340
Union links two leaders.
